  "query_preview": "..\n\n - Meteorological Overview:\n\nSouthwesterly upper level flow will be the predominant weather \npattern this week as upper level ridging stalls out over the \ncenter of the North American Continent this week, keeping us \ngenerally warm and dry for most of this week. Expect temperatures \nto generally be either side of 80 along the Missouri River \ncorridor, with temperatures not as warm in the higher terrain and \nalong the Rocky Mountain Front. Winds will also be \nuncharacteristically light for much of the week, with winds \ngenerally struggling to gust over 20 mph through Thursday. Aside \nfrom a stray shower or thunderstorm across Southwestern Montana \nTuesday or Wednesday afternoon, our next weather system will \narrive Friday or Saturday, which will bring cooler temperatures, \ngusty winds, and the possibility for some widespread \nprecipitation, though the amount of rainfall remains fairly \nuncertain at this point in time. \n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\n\nFriday/Saturday system: an upper level trough and associated\nsurface front embedded in the upper level flow are expected to\narrive in the Northern Rockies late Friday. Current model\nforecasts suggest that this setup will bring some widespread\nprecipitation, with the potential for many areas to receive over a\nquarter inch of liquid equivalent. The other concern will be the\nwind, as the strengthening trough may produce a bit of a\ndescending jet on its southwest side, which would produce some\ngusty westerly winds thanks to the sinking air behind a cold\nfront. As of Monday afternoon, probabilities for 60 mph wind gusts\nstill appear low (generally under 20 percent). However, wind gusts\nover 50 mph are likely to be common, and stronger wind gusts will\nbe heavily dependent on the timing and strength of this system.\nLudwig",
